#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bell_y

#% text
# `bell_y(n, k, xs)` gives the partial Bell polynomial B(n, k) in the variables `xs`; `bell_y(n, xs)` the complete Bell polynomial.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# B(n, k) sums n!/(prod (j!)^m_j m_j!) x_1^m_1 x_2^m_2 ... over the partitions of `n` into `k` parts (m_j parts equal to j).

#% item
# With `xs = [1, 1, ...]` it gives the Stirling numbers of the second kind; with `xs = [0!, -1!, 2!, ...]` the Stirling numbers of the first kind.

#% item
# `bell_y(m)` for a matrix `m` of one column is the complete Bell polynomial `bell_y(n, x)` of that column; for rows `[a_i, x_i]` it is the sum over `k` of `a_k` times `bell_y(n, k, x)`: `bell_y(constant_array([1, z], n))` is the Touchard polynomial, the sum of `z^k` times the Stirling numbers of the second kind.

#% item
# It is computed with the recurrence B(n, k) = sum over i of binomial(n - 1, i - 1) x_i B(n - i, k - 1) and expanded.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
bell_y(4, 2, [x1, x2, x3])
#% output
#= 3*x2^2 + 4*x1*x3

#% cell
bell_y(3, [x1, x2, x3])
#% output
#= x3 + 3*x1*x2 + x1^3

#% cell
bell_y([[x1], [x2], [x3]])
#% output
#= x3 + 3*x1*x2 + x1^3

#% cell
map(n => bell_y(constant_array([1, z], n)), range(1, 5))
#% output
#= [z, z + z^2, z + 3*z^2 + z^3, z + 7*z^2 + 6*z^3 + z^4, z + 15*z^2 + 25*z^3 + 10*z^4 + z^5]

#% cell
map(m => map(k => bell_y(m, k, constant_array(1, m)), range(0, m)), range(0, 5))
#% output
#= [[1], [0, 1], [0, 1, 1], [0, 1, 3, 1], [0, 1, 7, 6, 1], [0, 1, 15, 25, 10, 1]]

#% cell
bell_y(5, 3, [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]) == stirling_s2(5, 3)
#% output
#= true

